a man who won a landmark lawsuit against  the agriculture giant Monsanto is  speaking out for the first time only on  CBS this morning  Monsanto was ordered to pay 289 million  dollars in damages to former school  groundskeeper Dwayne Johnson the jury  found the company widely used glyphosate  based weed killer was a substantial  factor in causing Johnson's rare cancer  is the first of thousands of similar  lawsuits that the company faces across  the u.s. in a war nurse spoke with  Johnson and a good morning good morning  Nora he's the man who went up against  Monsanto and won but Dwayne Johnson who  goes by Lee says his lawsuit started  with a desire to warn others about the  potential dangers of chemicals that  glyphosate based weed killers he says  caused his terminal cancer to put it  bluntly you're dying yes but you say  it's not about you know ya dyin is  something that everybody has to do  alright so if you know you're dying it  gives you that extra push it's like okay  well you can't just die for nothing  y'all they don't know I am an integrated  pest management / grouseman Lee  Johnson's ordeal began when he took a  job as a Pest Control Manager for a Bay  Area School District starting in 2012  part of his job spraying glyphosate  based weed killers made by monsanto  chemicals he says trainers told him were  safe one of the things stuck out to me  the worst is that when they tell me it  was safe enough to Jane safe enough to  drink safe enough to drink that's that  was really absolutely it became the joke  almost like a Manitou drink it it was  like what you know it was really a shot  despite that Johnson says he always wore  his protective gear while spraying some  20 to 30 times a year sometimes two to  five hours a day are you gonna get it on  you you're gonna get it on your skin you  know the man there was never like oh I  didn't even get any on me today it was  always like man I'm glad I had this off  especially he says after a hose came  loose spraying chemicals inside his suit  it was a few months after that he  developed a rash that spread over his  body they never went away and he got  worse and worse and where  doctors diagnosed him with non-hodgkin  lymphoma cancer later that year he  called Monsanto what did you want to ask  them what I wanted to ask them you know  is it possible that maybe if someone got  this on their skin would they get sick  but they did rashes what they did  lymphoma and what did they say to you  you know she definitely said mr. Johnson  somebody will give you a call about this  it won't be me but somebody will call  you and will talk to you about did they  ever call you back no an email  introduced at trial shows an executive  was notified and wrote that he would  call Johnson back in a later deposition  the executive said he didn't recall  whether he'd ever spoken to Johnson so a  little over a year later in 2016 with  his health declining Johnson sued  earlier this month after an eight week  trial in nearly three days of  deliberations the jury stunned the world  with a 289 million dollar verdict  against Monsanto finding the company  failed to warn Johnson and other  consumers of the cancer risks what did  that verdict mean to you the verdict  really meant to me that this thing was  not done in vain and I remember standing  there saying to myself if I lose this  case this company is going to be able to  get away and they'll be able to say see  I told you our stuff didn't do that  Monsanto still says its products did not  cause Johnson's cancer after the verdict  executive vice president Scott partridge  said we all have tremendous sympathy for  mr. Johnson and his and his family what  they've gone through with his disease is  is terrible but of the verdict he said  it doesn't change the overwhelming  scientific evidence and the forty years  of safe use of glyphosate around the  world what do you make of the fact that  Monsanto is not backing down they have  to not back down is what I think you  know what I mean it's like if they just  say come out and say okay well okay you  got us that's the end of them  Monsanto promises to appeal Johnson may  not live to see the fine  outcome of his lawsuit but he says  knowing the end is near has allowed him  to pass on the lessons of his life to  his two sons I want my son's to know to  go forth and always do what they're  supposed to do and never be weak and be  strong all the way through but you gotta  have pain oh yeah the pain makes you  stronger  things like that happen but I think it  happens for a reason I'm still here I  was supposed to be going a long time ago  it's hard times always kind of up and  downs all these kinds of you deal with  that accordingly you move forward  Monsanto continues to say that 800  scientific studies and reviews including  a conclusion from the Environmental  Protection Agency support the fact that  glyphosate does not cause cancer but  Johnson hopes this verdict will lead to  labeling changes for these products so  he says more people will know the  potential risks and be able to choose  whether they want to use those products  he seems like a very humble man you know  you know it's amazing yeah to have that  outlook and he as he says move forward  and not feel down and depressed all the  time yeah he says he does get down but  he's got his family there and these sons  have a lot to be proud of in there does  he's had time to talk to his sons  because he knows the ends coming and it  could be very soon yeah it's important  that we're hearing from him Anna thank  you
 

